# runtime/APPROVED_BUILTINS
#
# Allowlist of every non-static `long long <name>(` function exported from
# sprout_runtime.c.  The pre-commit hook blocks any addition to that file
# whose name does not appear here.
#
# To add a new builtin you MUST:
#   1. Add its name to this file (one per line, leading/trailing whitespace ignored).
#   2. Add a comment on the SAME line explaining why it cannot be implemented
#      in Sprout stdlib or via composition of existing builtins.
#   3. Reference AGENTS.md "Builtin vs Stdlib" rules 4–6 in your commit message.
#
# Existing builtins are listed without individual justifications because they
# predate this policy.  New additions MUST include an inline comment.
#
# Format:  <function_name>  [# <justification for new entries>]
#
# DELIBERATELY ABSENT, so the omission does not read as an oversight:
#   double_to_bits / double_from_bits — raw IEEE 754 bit access, added 2026-08-07.
#     They are COMPILER INTRINSICS, not runtime functions: there is no
#     @double_to_bits symbol to allowlist. Under the i64-uniform value ABI a
#     Double and an Int are already the same LLVM type, so the bitcast lowers to
#     nothing at all — ast_to_ir returns the argument's SSA name unchanged. Same
#     arrangement as `to_double` and `print`, neither of which is listed here
#     either. See docs/double-bit-access-v0.md.
#   bit_and / bit_or / bit_xor / bit_not / bit_shl / bit_shr / bit_shr_zf —
#     stdlib.bits, added 2026-08-17. Also COMPILER INTRINSICS with no runtime
#     symbol: each lowers to one LLVM instruction (and/or/xor/shl/ashr/lshr;
#     bit_not is xor against -1), so there is nothing to call and nothing to
#     allowlist. Rule 4 does not even come up — this is not "impossible in
#     Sprout" but "not a function". The shift-count guard is emitted in
#     ast_to_ir and reuses the existing @panic. See docs/bitwise-int-ops-v0.md.

