tcp_write_all_timeout  # tcp_write_all bounded by an IDLE deadline: no single stall may exceed idle_ms, and any byte the kernel accepts re-arms the bound, after which it returns Err(TcpTimeout) with the connection still valid. Correctness, not performance, and the exact write-side counterpart of tcp_read_avail_timeout: an unbounded write is the same remote liveness hazard as an unbounded read. A client that requests a response larger than the socket buffers and then stops reading — without closing — parks its handler in send() forever, so the linear close never runs and the connection's slot in the 2048-entry handle table is never returned; 2048 such clients and tcp_accept serves nobody. Bounding the read alone left that reachable from the write side, including for the 408/431 replies, which travel the same path. NOT expressible in Sprout for the same reason as the read: the bound needs the scheduler to register one task on an fd AND a timer at once (scheduler_park_on_fd_timeout), and Sprout has no way to park. Idle rather than total follows nginx send_timeout ("the timeout is set only between two successive write operations, not for the transmission of the whole response"), so a slow-but-reading client is never cut off. idle_ms <= 0 attempts the write once without parking, matching tcp_read_avail_timeout. Scheduled for deletion: the tcp_wait readiness-primitive decomposition (see BACKLOG) replaces every tcp_*_timeout twin with ONE park primitive plus Sprout-level retry loops.
tcp_wait  # READINESS ONLY, moving no data: parks the calling task until the connection is ready for `interest` (read or write) or `ms` elapses; Ok(true) = ready, Ok(false) = deadline passed. Cannot be expressed in Sprout for the same reason the timed twins cannot: it needs the scheduler to register one task on an fd AND a timer at once (scheduler_park_on_fd_timeout), and Sprout has no way to park. This is the first half of the BACKLOG:483 decomposition — being interest-parameterised it is the ONLY park primitive read, write, connect and accept will ever need, which is what lets every `tcp_*_timeout` twin eventually be deleted rather than joined by a fourth. `ms <= 0` reports "not ready" without parking, so a caller enforcing a TOTAL budget can pass the remaining slice.
tcp_write_some  # TRANSFER ONLY, never parking: one send from `offset`, Ok(n>0) on progress, Err(TcpWouldBlock) when the kernel took nothing. Paired with tcp_wait by a Sprout-level loop. Not expressible in Sprout because a raw non-blocking send on an OS socket is a host effect. Justified now (not deferred to the full decomposition) because the response write is the remaining UNBOUNDED worker-occupancy path: tcp_write_all_timeout re-arms its idle bound in C on every progressing send, so a peer accepting one byte per interval holds a handler forever and no deadline computed in Sprout can intervene — Sprout never regains control mid-payload, and chunking does not help because the re-arm applies within a chunk too. Bounding it is the prerequisite for the bounded worker pool (docs/green-task-pool-v0.md §5.2). `offset` exists so the Sprout loop advances an index rather than re-slicing Bytes per chunk, which would make an n-byte response O(n^2) — the cost BACKLOG:483 flagged to design around.
tcp_read_some  # TRANSFER ONLY, never parking: one recv of at most max_bytes, Ok(Bytes) with >=1 byte on progress, Err(TcpWouldBlock) when the kernel had none, Err(TcpEndOfStream) on a clean peer close. Paired with tcp_wait by a Sprout-level loop; the exact read-side counterpart of tcp_write_some. Not expressible in Sprout because a raw non-blocking recv on an OS socket is a host effect. This REPLACES tcp_read, tcp_read_avail and tcp_read_avail_timeout (all three deleted), so it is a net reduction of three builtins to one. Two reasons it had to happen rather than waiting for the rest of the BACKLOG:483 decomposition. (1) SOUNDNESS: all three ended in sprout_gc_adopt_cstr(buf, n, ...), handing back a String built from unvalidated socket bytes, so any payload containing 0x00 violated the String invariant of docs/spec-v0.md:64 ('always valid UTF-8, contains no NUL byte') — W2 R2, whose fix was decided as D4 on 2026-07-04. Returning Bytes removes the mint; bytes.to_string is the single validating choke point and already rejects a NUL as well as invalid UTF-8. (2) The three welded timeout policy to syscall retry inside C, which is why each re-derived the same park/retry dance and drifted (tcp_read aborted the process where tcp_read_avail returned Err). Policy — total budget, size cap, minimum rate — is now ordinary Sprout in stdlib.net, where the typechecker and test suite can see it. max_bytes is clamped to 64 KiB; a short read is already the contract, so the clamp is unobservable.
__scope_open  # L0.1 structured concurrency: allocate a scheduler scope. Green-thread scheduling needs OS stack/context switching (ucontext) that cannot be expressed in Sprout.
__scope_join  # L0.1: block the current task until all tasks spawned into a scope finish. Requires driving the cooperative scheduler (context switching) — not expressible in Sprout.
__scope_spawn  # L0.1: create a fire-and-forget green task (own stack) running a Unit->Unit closure and enqueue it. Needs makecontext/stack allocation — not expressible in Sprout.
__task_fork  # L0.4: create an AWAITABLE green task running a Unit->a closure; returns a handle whose result is kept alive until awaited. Needs makecontext/stack allocation + per-task GC rooting of the result — not expressible in Sprout.
__task_await  # L0.4: block the current task until a forked task finishes, then return its result. Requires driving the cooperative scheduler (context switching) — not expressible in Sprout.
__scope_cancel  # L0.5: set a scope's cancelled flag (owner-only). Operates on scheduler-internal Scope state (owner check, cancelled flag) — not expressible in Sprout.
__task_stop_reason  # L0.7: read the current task's scope stop-reason (0 none / 1 cancelled / 2 timed-out). Reads scheduler-internal per-task/scope state — not expressible in Sprout. (task_cancelled + task_status are built on this in stdlib.task.)
__await_deadline  # L0.7 with_timeout: arm a deadline timer on the owner and drive the scheduler until a forked child finishes or the deadline fires (force-dropping the child). Requires kernel timer + context switching + per-task GC-rooted reclamation — not expressible in Sprout.
task_yield  # L0.1: cooperative yield — suspend the current task and switch to the scheduler. Context switching only, not expressible in Sprout.
__task_sleep  # L0.6: suspend the current task on a one-shot kqueue/epoll timer for ms milliseconds, running siblings meanwhile. Requires a kernel timer + driving the scheduler — not expressible in Sprout.
__chan_new  # L0.8 channels: allocate a bounded buffered channel in a scope, with its own GC-root context rooting every buffer slot. Scheduler-internal (Scope list, non-moving buffer, root registration) — not expressible in Sprout.
__chan_send  # L0.8: send a value into a channel; parks the current task (context switch) when the buffer is full, or hands off directly to a parked receiver. Requires driving the cooperative scheduler + GC-rooting the in-flight value across the park — not expressible in Sprout.
__chan_recv  # L0.8/L0.9: receive from a channel as `Recv a` (Got v | Closed); parks the current task (context switch) when empty and open, waking a parked sender to refill. Requires driving the cooperative scheduler — not expressible in Sprout.
__chan_close  # L0.9: close a channel (end-of-stream signal); wakes recv-parked tasks with Closed and aborts send-parked tasks. Manipulates scheduler wait-queues + parks/context switches — not expressible in Sprout.
__chan_select  # L0.11: wait on several channels at once (recv), returning which became ready first and its Recv outcome. Registers the current task on every channel's select wait-queue and parks (context switch) until one fires; unregisters from the rest atomically. Drives the cooperative scheduler across N channels — not expressible in Sprout.
